    Bug 521649 - Rewrite StripHtml to skip CSS and decode HTML entities during search indexing. r=mkmelin

    The previous implementation of `nsMsgBodyHandler::StripHtml` used a naive
    string loop that only deleted characters directly between `<` and `>`. This
    caused two major search indexing bugs:
    1. Invisible CSS/JS text leaked into the index (Bug 628098, searching "city"
       matched "opacity").
    2. HTML entities were not decoded (Bug 521649, searching "Münster" failed
       because the text held "M&uuml;nster").

    This patch replaces the naive loop with a high-speed, zero-allocation
    SAX-style parser:
    * Introduces a `SkipMode` state machine to completely ignore the contents of
      `<style>` and `<script>` blocks.
    * Implements a tiered HTML entity decoder using a static, ASCII-sorted
      lookup table and binary search.
    * Safely decodes structural entities (Big Five), Latin-1 supplements (ä, ö,
      ü), strips word-splitters (&shy;, &zwnj;), and converts word-mergers
      (&nbsp;) to spaces.
    * Performs all mutations in-place by ensuring `writePtr` safely trails
      `readPtr`, preventing memory allocation overhead during synchronous
      searches.
    * Uses modern Mozilla APIs (`nsDependentCSubstring`, `LowerCaseEqualsASCII`,
      `mozilla::IsAsciiAlpha`) with strict boundary checks to ensure memory safety.
    * Uses a strictly bounded `memchr` lookahead (max 12 bytes) for entity
      scanning, guaranteeing O(N) linear parsing and preventing potential
      algorithmic DoS on maliciously crafted emails.

    Includes a new test file (`html-entities-and-styles.eml`) and extends
    `test_searchBody.js` to verify CSS skipping and tiered entity translation.

    Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D292941

